WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:24.709 --> 00:00:25.709
[Informal Talk]

00:00:25.709 --> 00:00:38.629
I first met Richard Scrushy in the summer of 1980. I was

00:00:38.649 --> 00:00:42.909
living in Houston Texas and I had recently passed the CPA

00:00:42.929 --> 00:00:46.409
exam and I wanted to go to work for a large company, a

00:00:46.429 --> 00:00:50.379
publicly held company; I had always worked for rather small.

00:00:50.399 --> 00:00:54.399
And I answered an ad in the paper and it was for a

00:00:54.419 --> 00:00:58.759
controller position at Lifemark Corporation; Lifemark was a

00:00:58.779 --> 00:01:02.059
publicly held hospital company. And I went in for the

00:01:02.079 --> 00:01:06.469
interview and it was with Richard Scrushy and I had never

00:01:06.489 --> 00:01:09.750
met anybody quite like Richard. All during the interview my

00:01:09.769 --> 00:01:14.579
head was just spinning I just couldn't believe the person

00:01:14.599 --> 00:01:18.959
that he was and by the end of the interview I was totally

00:01:18.979 --> 00:01:22.079
convinced that that was the best job I could possibly get in

00:01:22.099 --> 00:01:26.140
Huston Texas. But as I was driving home I kept thinking

00:01:26.159 --> 00:01:29.519
about the interview and when I got home I told my wife I

00:01:29.539 --> 00:01:33.549
said I think today I have met the most brilliant businessman

00:01:33.569 --> 00:01:37.810
I am ever going to meet or may be the biggest con artist I

00:01:37.829 --> 00:01:41.909
will ever meet, and that's actually been quoted in the Wall

00:01:41.929 --> 00:01:44.959
Street Journal and the New York Times and a lot of other

00:01:44.979 --> 00:01:48.969
publications. But he did, he offered me the job, I reported

00:01:48.989 --> 00:01:52.739
to work. I got to work, my first day I was there about 30

00:01:52.759 --> 00:01:55.759
minutes early and he came into my office and he said Aaron,

00:01:55.759 --> 00:01:59.140
he said, I am presenting a contract to my boss and I would

00:01:59.159 --> 00:02:02.159
like you to sit in on my presentation, it's a contract I

00:02:02.159 --> 00:02:05.349
think we should sign. And I went in, he introduced me to his

00:02:05.349 --> 00:02:10.429
boss and he said Aaron and I worked on this contract for

00:02:10.449 --> 00:02:15.169
hours last night and I hadn't worked on anything, I had

00:02:15.189 --> 00:02:20.179
barely stepped in front door. And today, I realize that he

00:02:20.199 --> 00:02:25.119
probably told that lie to size me up, to see how I would

00:02:25.139 --> 00:02:28.979
react to being included in the lie. At that time I didn't

00:02:29.000 --> 00:02:32.799
think that much about it but I know more about Richard

00:02:32.819 --> 00:02:38.539
Scrushy now and I am sure that he told that lie on purpose.

00:02:38.560 --> 00:02:42.759
I worked for Richard for almost four years at Lifemark. We

00:02:42.780 --> 00:02:46.429
managed departments within hospitals. We would contract with

00:02:46.449 --> 00:02:49.949
hospital to run their respiratory therapy, physical therapy,

00:02:49.969 --> 00:02:53.969
and pharmacists and Richard was just amazing. He could go

00:02:53.989 --> 00:02:58.289
into a little hospital in Arkansas or somewhere and walk in

00:02:58.310 --> 00:03:02.339
and walk out with three contracts to run big revenue centers

00:03:02.359 --> 00:03:05.679
in their hospitals and would take a percent of the money.

00:03:05.699 --> 00:03:12.909
But I also learned that he had a real dark side, he was so

00:03:12.929 --> 00:03:17.239
into himself and I remember many times sitting in his office

00:03:17.259 --> 00:03:19.909
and he would be ranting and raving about a problem he was

00:03:19.909 --> 00:03:25.149
having with his ex-wife or his boss and it was always about

00:03:25.169 --> 00:03:31.879
Richard, never about me, always about him. In the summer of

00:03:31.899 --> 00:03:35.929
1983, I picked up the Wall Street Journal a morning, read

00:03:35.949 --> 00:03:40.459
and it said Lifemark to be acquired by AMI, a much larger

00:03:40.479 --> 00:03:44.159
hospital company out of California. And the article said

00:03:44.179 --> 00:03:50.009
they would be closing the offices of Lifemark and I thought

00:03:50.030 --> 00:03:53.530
oh no man, I am going to be without a job. But what happens

00:03:53.530 --> 00:03:56.030
many times, these were two New York stock exchange companies

00:03:56.030 --> 00:03:58.780
and a lot of times when they merge, a venture capitalist

00:03:58.780 --> 00:04:02.169
will approach, the company's being bought out and Lifemark

00:04:02.189 --> 00:04:06.329
was clearly being bought out and Citicorp Venture Capital

00:04:06.349 --> 00:04:09.929
contacted Bill Mackey, the chairman of the board, and asked

00:04:09.949 --> 00:04:13.329
him, does anybody have an idea for a startup company that

00:04:13.349 --> 00:04:17.470
they could back and Bill Mackey said Richard Scrushy without

00:04:17.490 --> 00:04:21.569
a doubt. So we made him a full VP when he was only 26 years

00:04:21.589 --> 00:04:25.340
old and he's been talking to us about getting into the

00:04:25.360 --> 00:04:29.259
outpatient business. Back in the early 80s if you had an

00:04:29.279 --> 00:04:34.810
accident or you needed physical therapy after a surgery, the

00:04:34.829 --> 00:04:37.920
physician would just keep you in the hospital as an in-

00:04:37.939 --> 00:04:41.720
patient and you would receive your therapy as an in-patient.

00:04:41.740 --> 00:04:44.680
Even back then the cost of health care was a big concern.

00:04:44.680 --> 00:04:48.590
Richard's concept was get the patient out of the hospital as

00:04:48.610 --> 00:04:52.170
fast as you can, or certainly eliminate the room and board

00:04:52.189 --> 00:04:55.660
portion of the hospital bill and the patient would rather go

00:04:55.680 --> 00:04:59.879
home and go back as an outpatient rather than staying in a

00:04:59.899 --> 00:05:03.170
building full of sick people and he felt like the therapist

00:05:03.189 --> 00:05:06.560
would rather work in that kind of environment rather than in

00:05:06.579 --> 00:05:10.310
the hospital itself. In a hospital, the doctors and the

00:05:10.329 --> 00:05:16.019
nurses are kind of the big dudes, the therapy departments

00:05:16.039 --> 00:05:18.959
are kind of off in the basement somewhere and don't get that

00:05:18.959 --> 00:05:22.350
much recognition and Richard felt like if you put them in

00:05:22.370 --> 00:05:26.680
their own freestanding outpatient center they will like

00:05:26.699 --> 00:05:30.970
working there better. He convinced Citicorp to put a million

00:05:30.990 --> 00:05:34.470
dollars into his startup company and he wanted me to be his

00:05:34.490 --> 00:05:39.680
CFO to help build a company that hopefully one day would go

00:05:39.699 --> 00:05:44.300
public. But I didn't want to, I had had about enough of

00:05:44.319 --> 00:05:49.600
Richard. He just glares down on you and he manages by

00:05:49.620 --> 00:05:53.019
intimidation and I really kind of wanted to get away from

00:05:53.039 --> 00:05:57.800
him even at that point. But he said look Aaron, he said put

00:05:57.819 --> 00:06:02.579
in $5,000 you would get a 100,000 shares of stock, nickel a

00:06:02.599 --> 00:06:06.160
share. He said I am putting in $25,000 I am going to get

00:06:06.180 --> 00:06:09.819
500,000 shares and you have got to realize Citicorp is

00:06:09.839 --> 00:06:14.220
paying a dollar a share for their million shares so today

00:06:14.240 --> 00:06:18.379
your nickel is already worth a dollar. And I didn't have a

00:06:18.399 --> 00:06:23.350
job, I was going to be laid off and I believed Richard could

00:06:23.370 --> 00:06:27.399
do it, I was impressed that he got Citicorp to put a million

00:06:27.419 --> 00:06:31.459
dollar in him, and it was almost on the back of an envelope,

00:06:31.479 --> 00:06:34.290
they had already cut the deal, no formal business plan or

00:06:34.310 --> 00:06:38.920
anything. And I felt like he really could run a big company

00:06:38.939 --> 00:06:43.350
and take it public so I said okay I am in.

00:06:43.370 --> 00:06:46.470
Richard was from Alabama. He was born and raised in Selma

00:06:46.490 --> 00:06:51.040
Alabama so he wanted to start the company up in Birmingham.

00:06:51.060 --> 00:06:53.800
And we moved to Birmingham and we opened our first

00:06:53.819 --> 00:06:57.329
outpatient center, and it looked more like a fitness center

00:06:57.349 --> 00:07:01.029
or a spa or something and that was part of our concept was

00:07:01.049 --> 00:07:04.949
to make it non-medical as much as possible so that the

00:07:04.969 --> 00:07:08.319
patient feels like they are going to a spa or something

00:07:08.339 --> 00:07:11.980
rather than getting medical treatment. We opened our first

00:07:12.000 --> 00:07:14.829
center and it started seeing patients right away, we charged

00:07:14.849 --> 00:07:21.120
less than the acute care hospitals and we were off and

00:07:21.139 --> 00:07:23.569
running. And we raised additional venture capital, opened

00:07:23.589 --> 00:07:32.350
more centers. I came in one morning, there were probably

00:07:32.370 --> 00:07:36.740
only maybe 15 people in the corporate office and Richard had

00:07:36.759 --> 00:07:41.519
drawn this stick figure image up here and he said look,

00:07:41.539 --> 00:07:45.889
these guys are pulling the wagon, he said a lot of you aren'

00:07:45.909 --> 00:07:49.569
t really hooked up, some of you are riding in the wagon,

00:07:49.589 --> 00:07:52.319
this guy is pulling the wagon backwards, this guy is just

00:07:52.319 --> 00:07:56.290
kind of fat and dumb sitting on the sidelines and he says I

00:07:56.310 --> 00:08:01.079
need you to all pull the wagon. And he left that drawing up

00:08:01.099 --> 00:08:05.009
and it became kind of a company motto, to pull the wagon.

00:08:05.029 --> 00:08:09.889
And he had it reproduced and as years went by, it was famed

00:08:09.909 --> 00:08:13.699
and it was in the lobby of every HealthSouth facility next

00:08:13.719 --> 00:08:18.969
to a picture of Richard Scrushy. And at the end of the year

00:08:18.989 --> 00:08:22.779
if you were an outstanding employee, you got 100 shares of

00:08:22.799 --> 00:08:26.560
stock and a little red wagon and it was called Pulling The

00:08:26.579 --> 00:08:31.050
Wagon Award. We discovered as we started to open these

00:08:31.069 --> 00:08:34.029
outpatients centers that there was a need for rehabilitation

00:08:34.049 --> 00:08:39.110
hospitals, specialty hospitals where you don't go for

00:08:39.129 --> 00:08:42.259
surgery, you don't go there if you are sick, you go there if

00:08:42.259 --> 00:08:45.509
you are really hurt or you have had a bad stroke and you

00:08:45.509 --> 00:08:49.470
still need nursing care so you are in-patient in a hospital

00:08:49.490 --> 00:08:53.779
but a rehab hospital. We also discovered that outpatient

00:08:53.799 --> 00:08:58.019
surgery was becoming a big business. Years ago a physician

00:08:58.039 --> 00:09:01.439
would not cut on you if you were not in an acute care

00:09:01.459 --> 00:09:05.149
hospital. But as you are probably aware, things have

00:09:05.169 --> 00:09:08.610
changed, technology has changed and a large percentage of

00:09:08.629 --> 00:09:12.659
all surgeries now are done on outpatient basis, and we got

00:09:12.679 --> 00:09:16.169
into that business. So we really had three basic businesses

00:09:16.189 --> 00:09:21.019
rehab, outpatient centers, hospitals and then outpatient

00:09:21.039 --> 00:09:27.939
surgery. We started the company in 1984 and by 1986 we were

00:09:27.959 --> 00:09:32.819
talking to investment bankers about going public. And I

00:09:32.839 --> 00:09:36.200
remember very well we were meeting with the bankers from

00:09:36.220 --> 00:09:40.480
Drexel Burnham; you may not know and the older people in the

00:09:40.500 --> 00:09:44.779
room would know about Drexel Burnham. But we were meeting

00:09:44.799 --> 00:09:47.509
with the banker and we were discussing our numbers and

00:09:47.529 --> 00:09:51.659
everything and he said look, you almost have a lot of

00:09:51.679 --> 00:09:54.429
startup calls so he said, how are you handling your startup

00:09:54.429 --> 00:10:00.840
calls? And I said well I am expensing it, very conservative

00:10:00.860 --> 00:10:04.169
accounting, just writing it off as we incurred it. And he

00:10:04.189 --> 00:10:07.439
looked at Richard and I and he almost had a twinkle in his

00:10:07.449 --> 00:10:10.449
eye and he said oh no, he said you should be capitalizing

00:10:10.449 --> 00:10:13.699
those calls. He said, I think I can get you public just as

00:10:13.699 --> 00:10:16.699
soon as you show a profit because you have a great concept

00:10:16.699 --> 00:10:21.240
but he said you ought to be capitalizing those expenses and

00:10:21.259 --> 00:10:24.539
get profitable quickly and we will take you public. And

00:10:24.559 --> 00:10:27.529
Richard looked at me and he said Aaron, why are you letting

00:10:27.529 --> 00:10:31.110
their accounting tail wag the dog, he says your blankety

00:10:31.129 --> 00:10:35.200
blank conservative accounting is holding us back and he said

00:10:35.220 --> 00:10:38.909
you should be doing this. He said, I can't believe you are

00:10:38.929 --> 00:10:42.129
hurting me, I am trying to get this company public and your

00:10:42.149 --> 00:10:44.840
conservative accounting is holding us back. And that's kind

00:10:44.860 --> 00:10:48.049
of how Richard managed; if he could get you down, he would

00:10:48.069 --> 00:10:52.409
really beat on you so to speak. So I re-stated our numbers,

00:10:52.429 --> 00:10:56.149
redid our projections and sure enough in the second quarter

00:10:56.169 --> 00:11:01.429
of 1986 we made a profit and the banker said let's go. So we

00:11:01.449 --> 00:11:04.449
went on a road show, we went to New York, Boston, Chicago,

00:11:04.449 --> 00:11:10.129
San Francisco, LA all of the big institutional buyers in

00:11:10.149 --> 00:11:14.350
these cities and we had our road show and we presented the

00:11:14.370 --> 00:11:18.069
stock to investors. The last road show was typically in New

00:11:18.089 --> 00:11:22.819
York City. The night before you priced the stock or the day

00:11:22.839 --> 00:11:26.669
before at luncheon, Richard made this just outstanding

00:11:26.689 --> 00:11:31.840
presentation and at the end of this presentation he got a

00:11:31.860 --> 00:11:34.860
standing ovation, and the guy sitting next to him, he said I

00:11:34.860 --> 00:11:37.360
have never seen anything like this. He said, normally people

00:11:37.360 --> 00:11:41.610
don't applaud to these types of presentations and he says,

00:11:41.629 --> 00:11:47.039
all my years on Wall Street I have never seen anyone get a

00:11:47.059 --> 00:11:49.809
standing ovation. And he said, you guys should not be going

00:11:49.809 --> 00:11:53.610
public, your company is only two years old, you top-line

00:11:53.629 --> 00:11:58.299
[inaudible] is only $5 million, you and Richard have no

00:11:58.319 --> 00:12:02.159
experience running a public company, there are no other

00:12:02.179 --> 00:12:04.809
companies quite like you out there so the analysts are going

00:12:04.809 --> 00:12:09.549
to have trouble writing research on you. But he said,

00:12:09.569 --> 00:12:12.439
clearly you are going to go public, Richard is such a

00:12:12.459 --> 00:12:17.419
salesman he will pull it off. Sure enough, we were priced to

00:12:17.439 --> 00:12:22.199
go out at $8 to $10 a share and the deal got done at 650, we

00:12:22.219 --> 00:12:28.049
barely made it, it was almost a pull at the last minute. But

00:12:28.069 --> 00:12:31.799
we got public, began trading and it was very exciting. I was

00:12:31.819 --> 00:12:34.909
calling my broker every 15 minutes to see how the stock was

00:12:34.929 --> 00:12:40.289
doing and within a few months we were a $10 stock. You can

00:12:40.309 --> 00:12:43.819
do the arithmetic I had a 100,000 shares, I am now a

00:12:43.839 --> 00:12:48.539
millionaire. And the money changed me. As the years went by,

00:12:48.559 --> 00:12:52.860
I bought a condo in the French Quarter, I bought a beach

00:12:52.879 --> 00:12:56.860
house in Florida. Over the years I bought $30,000 worth of

00:12:56.879 --> 00:13:01.250
these Hermes Neckties, they are really good looking neckties

00:13:01.269 --> 00:13:06.120
I have to admit, but I went kind of crazy on that. And it

00:13:06.139 --> 00:13:09.620
certainly changed Richard; Richard's ego now was on

00:13:09.639 --> 00:13:14.679
steroids. He formed a rock and roll band called Proxy and he

00:13:14.699 --> 00:13:19.600
began playing at company functions and even at music

00:13:19.620 --> 00:13:23.879
festivals and nightclubs and he wanted to become a rock

00:13:23.899 --> 00:13:27.990
star. And the band didn't do that well so he formed a

00:13:28.009 --> 00:13:32.449
country band and he went to Nashville and he hired musicians

00:13:32.469 --> 00:13:36.600
to back him and he actually wrote a song he called Honk If

00:13:36.620 --> 00:13:41.549
You Love the Honky Tonk. And he sent memos out to all of our

00:13:41.569 --> 00:13:45.689
employees asking them to call the radio stations and

00:13:45.709 --> 00:13:51.110
recommend his song be played, and he was just unbelievable.

00:13:51.129 --> 00:13:54.899
He also started always carrying a gun in his briefcase and

00:13:54.919 --> 00:13:58.269
he hired bodyguards that followed him everywhere and even if

00:13:58.289 --> 00:14:02.049
he went to Wal-Mart or whatever, his bodyguards would be

00:14:02.069 --> 00:14:05.629
behind him speaking into their sleeves and he almost became

00:14:05.649 --> 00:14:08.509
kind of a Hannibal Lecter type kind of person. He didn't

00:14:08.509 --> 00:14:14.240
look little bit like Hannibal Lecter but it was just amazing

00:14:14.259 --> 00:14:19.199
what the money did to him. In spite of this though, he was

00:14:19.219 --> 00:14:25.389
the darling of Wall Street. He had started an entire niche

00:14:25.409 --> 00:14:30.090
in health care and everybody wanted him on their board of

00:14:30.110 --> 00:14:33.840
directors, venture capitalists brought him other deals to

00:14:33.860 --> 00:14:40.799
invest in, he just absolutely was a darling of Wall Street.

00:14:40.819 --> 00:14:46.039
And he became very wealthy. He controlled the board of

00:14:46.059 --> 00:14:50.439
directors so when I got 10000 options he got a million that

00:14:50.459 --> 00:14:57.079
kind of a thing. In 1995 less than 10 years after starting

00:14:57.099 --> 00:15:03.539
the company I estimated from his publicly owned stock in

00:15:03.559 --> 00:15:07.850
HealthSouth and other companies he was worth $600 million.

00:15:07.870 --> 00:15:12.850
And he told the Birmingham News newspaper that he wanted to

00:15:12.870 --> 00:15:18.929
be a billionaire and he was well on his way. That was his

00:15:18.949 --> 00:15:24.059
goal to be as rich as he could be, to be a billionaire.

00:15:24.079 --> 00:15:27.419
HealthSouth did very well, we did lots of public offerings,

00:15:27.439 --> 00:15:32.539
about six companies went public in the same business we were

00:15:32.559 --> 00:15:36.899
in and we began buying up our competitors. We did pooling of

00:15:36.919 --> 00:15:40.350
interest, we would buy their stock with our stock and by

00:15:40.370 --> 00:15:45.750
1995, we were the largest company in Alabama, State of

00:15:45.769 --> 00:15:52.629
Alabama, we were operating in all 50 states, we had 50,000

00:15:52.649 --> 00:15:57.579
employees, we were a Fortune 500 Company and I was a rock-

00:15:57.599 --> 00:16:00.589
star in Birmingham. I could go into any restaurant and

00:16:00.609 --> 00:16:03.990
people would point at me, want to talk to me, it was just

00:16:04.009 --> 00:16:09.649
amazing. And it was quite the lifestyle. We started buying

00:16:09.669 --> 00:16:12.899
jet airplanes, we had two Gulfstream airplanes which were

00:16:12.919 --> 00:16:17.259
about $30 million airplanes. Many mornings, Richard and I

00:16:17.279 --> 00:16:20.419
would go to our hanger, get on our Gulfstream to whatever

00:16:20.439 --> 00:16:24.059
stirred us, cook us breakfast as we flew in Teterboro

00:16:24.079 --> 00:16:26.850
Airport, New Jersey. We would get off the airplane, there

00:16:26.870 --> 00:16:30.069
would be a helicopter waiting for us, we would get on the

00:16:30.089 --> 00:16:32.620
helicopter, fly into Manhattan, there would be a limo

00:16:32.639 --> 00:16:35.639
waiting for us. We would go to the Plaza Hotel where Charlie

00:16:35.659 --> 00:16:41.240
Sheen hangs out and make presentations to investors and then

00:16:41.259 --> 00:16:43.759
fly back home that night, never going through Atlanta or

00:16:43.779 --> 00:16:50.819
anything. It was really quite the lifestyle. Richard's

00:16:50.839 --> 00:16:54.730
obsession with being wealthy was unbelievable and he was the

00:16:54.750 --> 00:16:57.549
one that really talked to the analysts and investment

00:16:57.569 --> 00:17:03.509
bankers about what kind of earnings we could achieve. And he

00:17:03.529 --> 00:17:08.250
understood that his wealth was tied to Wall Street. And he

00:17:08.269 --> 00:17:11.519
would ask the analysts what do we need to do for you ought

00:17:11.519 --> 00:17:14.769
to keep a strong buy on the stock and they would tell him,

00:17:14.769 --> 00:17:18.920
you need to grow 30% whatever and he said we can do that.

00:17:18.940 --> 00:17:24.470
Everything was okay for the first 7, 8, 9 years, we would

00:17:24.490 --> 00:17:28.149
hit those what he can promised to the Street. But it got

00:17:28.169 --> 00:17:31.470
difficult. We were a big company now, there weren't as many

00:17:31.490 --> 00:17:36.680
growth opportunities and just compound growth of 20%-30% is

00:17:36.700 --> 00:17:40.349
difficult to achieve year after year after year. So we

00:17:40.369 --> 00:17:45.079
started doing some not-so-good accounting. In health care

00:17:45.099 --> 00:17:49.139
the biggest issue, the trick is revenue recognition which it

00:17:49.159 --> 00:17:53.079
is in many businesses. You have contracts with HMOs,

00:17:53.099 --> 00:17:58.940
Medicare, private companies, you have lot of bad debt so

00:17:58.960 --> 00:18:01.960
what you charge the patient and what you eventually collect

00:18:01.980 --> 00:18:05.750
are very different. So you have to make adjustments to that

00:18:05.769 --> 00:18:09.289
revenue to come up with a net receivable number that's a

00:18:09.309 --> 00:18:13.730
good number. As it became difficult to make our numbers

00:18:13.750 --> 00:18:18.029
maybe we mistreat estimates by a penny, we would say now

00:18:18.049 --> 00:18:22.519
maybe bad debts aren't 10% of revenue they are going to be

00:18:22.539 --> 00:18:26.629
8% and we would change our basis for the accounting for bad

00:18:26.649 --> 00:18:32.500
debts. Not fraud but certainly not good accounting. For

00:18:32.519 --> 00:18:35.889
those of you who are accounting majors you understand you

00:18:35.909 --> 00:18:38.639
don't change your underlying accounting principles to

00:18:38.659 --> 00:18:44.149
achieve a number, you change those principles when it really

00:18:44.169 --> 00:18:47.930
dictates the circumstances that say you should. But we did

00:18:47.950 --> 00:18:54.899
it to make our numbers. Everything was fine until 1996, 10

00:18:54.919 --> 00:18:59.069
years of being a public company. And we had completed our

00:18:59.089 --> 00:19:01.930
second quarter and we had missed our numbers really badly

00:19:01.950 --> 00:19:06.289
and the Street was picking up that our days

00:19:06.309 --> 00:19:08.899
[inaudible] were growing, our cash flow didn't seem to be

00:19:08.899 --> 00:19:12.509
what it should be and Bill Owens who was my chief accountant

00:19:12.529 --> 00:19:16.309
now just felt like we could not play with the reserves

00:19:16.329 --> 00:19:21.129
anymore, it would be too transparent and it was time to

00:19:21.149 --> 00:19:25.359
report a bad quarter. We went into Richard's office and we

00:19:25.379 --> 00:19:30.450
showed him the numbers and we told him and he said no, have

00:19:30.470 --> 00:19:35.059
you guys lost your minds, he said we can't do that, he said

00:19:35.079 --> 00:19:38.079
the analysts are going to put sales on the stock, it's going

00:19:38.079 --> 00:19:40.950
to crash, we are going to be sued by stockholders, your

00:19:40.970 --> 00:19:44.139
stock options are going to be worthless, you are not going

00:19:44.159 --> 00:19:47.259
to be the rock stars in Birmingham anymore. He said we can't

00:19:47.259 --> 00:19:53.259
do that. And he said look, and he is a cold bloke, he put on

00:19:53.279 --> 00:19:56.389
his salesman hat and he said now look, health care is the

00:19:56.409 --> 00:19:59.440
biggest business in the United States, we are a small part

00:19:59.460 --> 00:20:02.619
of it. He said, we can get into the acute care hospital

00:20:02.639 --> 00:20:06.670
business and become if not the largest one of the largest

00:20:06.690 --> 00:20:11.170
companies in the country but he said I can't do it if you

00:20:11.190 --> 00:20:15.269
all screw this up by reporting these bad numbers. And he

00:20:15.289 --> 00:20:18.409
said you have all done things to the numbers before you know

00:20:18.429 --> 00:20:21.470
capitalizing calls, changing estimates, he said there's got

00:20:21.490 --> 00:20:24.909
to be something you can do to get us past this little bump

00:20:24.929 --> 00:20:29.869
in the road. And Bill Owens who was my chief accountant who

00:20:29.889 --> 00:20:36.119
had worked for auditors said Richard, he said look, we have

00:20:36.139 --> 00:20:41.309
1500 general ledgers, he said I can make entries small

00:20:41.329 --> 00:20:46.029
enough that the auditors won't look at them, and he was very

00:20:46.049 --> 00:20:50.200
clear he said, I will be crediting revenue that don't exist

00:20:50.220 --> 00:20:56.299
and debiting assets that don't exist. I was intimidated by

00:20:56.319 --> 00:21:00.430
Richard, I am ashamed to admit it but I did not have the

00:21:00.450 --> 00:21:04.750
courage to stand up to him at that point. I knew he had a

00:21:04.769 --> 00:21:08.879
gun in his briefcase, I had seen him get so angry about

00:21:08.899 --> 00:21:13.539
small things that I could not think of causing his net worth

00:21:13.559 --> 00:21:17.849
to go down by several hundred million dollars. And I

00:21:17.869 --> 00:21:20.169
believed in the company, we were a good company, and I

00:21:20.169 --> 00:21:25.019
wanted a part of it to keep going on and I will be honest

00:21:25.039 --> 00:21:29.559
with you I did not want to report bad numbers either. So

00:21:29.579 --> 00:21:36.210
that night Bill Owens cooked the books. I told my wife about

00:21:36.230 --> 00:21:40.720
it when I went home and she told me how stupid I was and

00:21:40.740 --> 00:21:43.919
that night of course I couldn't sleep. I woke up the next

00:21:43.919 --> 00:21:47.389
day and I felt like I had to put a genie back in the bottle

00:21:47.389 --> 00:21:51.569
and of course I couldn't and that point in my life changed

00:21:51.589 --> 00:21:57.359
and I was now living a lie, we had cooked the books. The

00:21:57.379 --> 00:22:01.889
next quarters rolls around and we do it again, not quite as

00:22:01.909 --> 00:22:07.649
difficult the second time. 1996 ends, I lie to the auditors

00:22:07.669 --> 00:22:10.769
when they ask sort of standard questions about, have any

00:22:10.789 --> 00:22:13.500
interest been made or not made that should have been made

00:22:13.500 --> 00:22:16.960
and sure enough, we got it by the auditors and it wasn't

00:22:16.980 --> 00:22:23.659
discovered. 1997 begins and we plead with Richard to lower

00:22:23.679 --> 00:22:28.909
Street expectations and he had no part it. He knew that once

00:22:28.929 --> 00:22:33.089
you are a high PE, high growth company, once you tell the

00:22:33.109 --> 00:22:36.889
Street you are not going to keep growing, they will put

00:22:36.909 --> 00:22:42.269
sales or lease holds on the stock rather than strong buys.

00:22:42.289 --> 00:22:46.879
So we began 1997 and it was apparent to me that we were

00:22:46.899 --> 00:22:50.649
going to have another year of cooking the books because we

00:22:50.669 --> 00:22:54.419
were in a hole, we had to get out of the hole and then grow

00:22:54.419 --> 00:22:57.339
unrealistically. During this period of time I think it was

00:22:57.359 --> 00:23:03.259
early 1997, Richard told Bill Owens and I, he said if we are

00:23:03.279 --> 00:23:09.589
ever caught, I will deny everything. You guys do what you

00:23:09.609 --> 00:23:15.809
want but I am telling you I am going to deny everything.

00:23:15.829 --> 00:23:19.899
Well my life is an utter mess. I am beginning to drink more

00:23:19.919 --> 00:23:24.859
than I should, I hate my job, I hate going to work everyday

00:23:24.879 --> 00:23:29.419
and I felt trapped and I didn't want to be a whistleblower.

00:23:29.439 --> 00:23:32.470
With Richard making that statement, I knew he would bring

00:23:32.490 --> 00:23:36.379
more lawyers, guns and money to the party than I would, I

00:23:36.399 --> 00:23:40.089
didn't want to stay because it was not fun anymore, it was

00:23:40.109 --> 00:23:44.759
all a lie. So I went to Richard and I told him I wanted to

00:23:44.779 --> 00:23:49.079
retire and I was only 54 years old and he said, if that's

00:23:49.099 --> 00:23:52.419
what you want to do, he said I think you are making a

00:23:52.439 --> 00:23:55.200
mistake but if that's what you want to do, and we never

00:23:55.200 --> 00:23:57.950
talked about the fraud and Richard is very smart, you don't

00:23:57.950 --> 00:24:01.029
have to spell things out to him. So I left the company in

00:24:01.029 --> 00:24:08.579
1997 and I moved to South Alabama Coast and about as soon as

00:24:08.599 --> 00:24:14.379
I was not an insider anymore, I sold lot of my stock and I

00:24:14.399 --> 00:24:19.480
bought 25 acres of land and I built a very nice house and

00:24:19.500 --> 00:24:24.369
for some reason I built a football field in my backyard and

00:24:24.389 --> 00:24:27.569
to this day I don't know of anyone else that built, I mean

00:24:27.569 --> 00:24:35.309
had a goalpost and everything. And I just distanced myself

00:24:35.329 --> 00:24:39.919
from the company I just put it out of my mind. But one day

00:24:39.939 --> 00:24:43.190
about a year after I retired Richard called me and said come

00:24:43.210 --> 00:24:46.079
to Birmingham, have lunch with me. So I went to Birmingham

00:24:46.099 --> 00:24:50.190
and went into the dining room of the corporate offices and

00:24:50.210 --> 00:24:52.939
he said look Aaron, I need you to come back to work. He said

00:24:52.939 --> 00:24:55.960
that we are making our numbers fine now, he said everything

00:24:55.980 --> 00:24:59.700
is okay, you need to come back and help me grow the company.

00:24:59.720 --> 00:25:03.339
And I told him no, I really did not want to get back into

00:25:03.359 --> 00:25:07.309
the frying pan and I was having lot of fun with my dogs and

00:25:07.329 --> 00:25:10.769
my football field. So I went back and 1998 passes 1999,

00:25:10.789 --> 00:25:24.039
2000, 2001, 2002 but in the spring of 2003 I am watching the

00:25:24.059 --> 00:25:28.029
evening news and they open up with we have a breaking story

00:25:28.049 --> 00:25:32.179
out of Birmingham Alabama, massive accounting fraud

00:25:32.199 --> 00:25:37.329
uncovered at HealthSouth, billions of bogus entries have

00:25:37.349 --> 00:25:42.319
been made to the books and I was like God. When I first

00:25:42.339 --> 00:25:45.470
retired, every time my doorbell rang or my phone rang, I

00:25:45.490 --> 00:25:50.730
thought it was FBI but it had been 6 years and I wanted to

00:25:50.750 --> 00:25:56.730
believe the fraud had stopped. So I am sure I wasn't as

00:25:56.750 --> 00:26:00.549
surprised as the average guy on the street but I was amazed

00:26:00.569 --> 00:26:05.299
that the fraud had gone on all those years. Alice Martin who

00:26:05.319 --> 00:26:07.740
was the attorney with the federal government out of the

00:26:07.740 --> 00:26:10.480
Northern District of Alabama in the newspaper the next day

00:26:10.500 --> 00:26:14.359
said several people have come forward, admitted their

00:26:14.379 --> 00:26:16.960
involvement in the fraud but there are others that are

00:26:16.980 --> 00:26:20.309
involved and if you are one of those people, you need to

00:26:20.329 --> 00:26:23.619
come forward and I almost thought she was going to name me

00:26:23.639 --> 00:26:28.700
by name in paper. But my wife tried to console me and say

00:26:28.720 --> 00:26:32.059
look, you have been gone a long time and the fraud wasn't

00:26:32.079 --> 00:26:35.079
that big when you were there, they are not going to come

00:26:35.079 --> 00:26:40.109
after you, and I said I don't think so. So I contacted an

00:26:40.129 --> 00:26:44.059
attorney, a white collar criminal attorney and he knew Alice

00:26:44.079 --> 00:26:46.899
Martin's people, he made some phone calls and he called me

00:26:46.899 --> 00:26:50.399
back and he said oh yeah, Mr. Beam you need to come talk to

00:26:50.399 --> 00:26:54.279
me. So my wife and I went in and the first thing he said, he

00:26:54.299 --> 00:26:57.909
says do not lie to me and do not lie to the federal

00:26:57.929 --> 00:27:01.819
government. He says your former employees have told them you

00:27:01.839 --> 00:27:06.679
were involved, the FBI is all over that building, and if you

00:27:06.699 --> 00:27:09.869
lie, they are going to come down on you like a freight

00:27:09.889 --> 00:27:14.490
train, you need to tell the truth. By now my wife is in

00:27:14.509 --> 00:27:19.500
tears, I am almost in tears and I have a kind of funny sense

00:27:19.519 --> 00:27:22.519
of humor, I asked him if he needed a check or something and

00:27:22.519 --> 00:27:26.019
he said yes, I would like for you to write me a check for

00:27:26.019 --> 00:27:30.230
$100,000. I said you got to be kidding. He said no, Mr.

00:27:30.250 --> 00:27:33.299
Beam, I am not kidding, he said that won't be the last check

00:27:33.299 --> 00:27:36.799
you are going to have to write. And I asked him if I could

00:27:36.799 --> 00:27:40.899
get a t-shirt or something and he gave me a coffee mug and

00:27:40.919 --> 00:27:43.789
my wife and I refer to it as a $100,000 coffee mug. Three

00:27:43.789 --> 00:27:49.639
days later I am in a federal building in Birmingham Alabama

00:27:49.659 --> 00:27:54.149
and I am sitting across the table from two FBI agents, two

00:27:54.169 --> 00:27:58.109
agents from the SEC, several attorneys from Alice Martin's

00:27:58.129 --> 00:28:02.899
office and I wasn't scared but it is not fun to be

00:28:02.919 --> 00:28:07.319
interrogated by the FBI and the SEC. And I felt like I didn'

00:28:07.339 --> 00:28:11.829
t do a good job because they wanted to know in 1996 when you

00:28:11.849 --> 00:28:15.039
first cooked the books what did you say to Richard, what did

00:28:15.059 --> 00:28:18.480
he say to you and they were recording all this and I couldn'

00:28:18.500 --> 00:28:22.509
t remember, I couldn't remember details. But at the end of

00:28:22.529 --> 00:28:28.700
the day, it lasted all day, FBI agent said Mr. Beam he said

00:28:28.720 --> 00:28:32.139
we know a lot about this case and from what we know we

00:28:32.159 --> 00:28:35.619
believe you were very truthful today and he said we

00:28:35.639 --> 00:28:41.099
appreciate that. Alice Martin's people said 17 people have

00:28:41.119 --> 00:28:44.919
come forward admitted their involvement in the fraud only

00:28:44.939 --> 00:28:50.419
one key person denies it, Richard Scrushy, he says he knows

00:28:50.439 --> 00:28:53.609
nothing about it and we are going to have take Mr. Scrushy

00:28:53.629 --> 00:28:57.509
to trial and we will like for you to be a witness. And they

00:28:57.529 --> 00:29:00.279
explained that they weren't cutting me a deal that it wasn't

00:29:00.279 --> 00:29:03.529
like rat Richard up and you don't go to prison, all we can

00:29:03.529 --> 00:29:09.359
tell you that if you cooperate, we recommend our senate, to

00:29:09.379 --> 00:29:12.429
a federal judge, we will ask him to take that into

00:29:12.449 --> 00:29:18.990
consideration. This is the spring of 2002 the trial didn't

00:29:19.009 --> 00:29:23.159
began until '05 in January. So I had a two year period where

00:29:23.179 --> 00:29:28.429
my life was literally very, very miserable. I knew I could

00:29:28.449 --> 00:29:33.579
possibly go to prison for north of 20 years basically rest

00:29:33.599 --> 00:29:37.019
of my life. And my attorney explained to me that they are

00:29:37.039 --> 00:29:39.899
going to look at your personal balance sheet and because of

00:29:39.919 --> 00:29:42.329
the magnitude of the fraud, they can fine you whatever they

00:29:42.329 --> 00:29:47.109
want to fine you. And they are not going to put in a box

00:29:47.129 --> 00:29:49.879
underneath a freeway but you are not going to have a

00:29:49.879 --> 00:29:52.629
football field and a million dollar home and a home in

00:29:52.629 --> 00:29:56.009
Florida and all that, you are going to have to sell that in

00:29:56.029 --> 00:30:01.919
order to pay the government. So like I say it was very, very

00:30:01.939 --> 00:30:06.210
dark period of time. During this period of time, Richard

00:30:06.230 --> 00:30:09.939
Scrushy went on the offensive. He left the church that he

00:30:09.959 --> 00:30:13.599
belonged to and he joined an intercity church and he gave

00:30:13.619 --> 00:30:17.539
that church a million dollars. He also bought a television

00:30:17.559 --> 00:30:22.069
station in Birmingham and he and his wife every morning

00:30:22.089 --> 00:30:27.349
Monday through Friday preached the gospel on TV and he was a

00:30:27.369 --> 00:30:34.000
preacher. He spent $20 million on his lawyers. He hired a

00:30:34.019 --> 00:30:37.970
jury selection firm, he had a publicist and all during the

00:30:37.990 --> 00:30:42.669
trial everyday he always had a Bible in his hand, he and his

00:30:42.689 --> 00:30:47.319
wife and he was surrounded by ministers. I thought it was

00:30:47.339 --> 00:30:53.059
laughable, it was so obvious what he was trying to do I just

00:30:53.079 --> 00:30:56.389
didn't think it would work. The government wanted me to be

00:30:56.409 --> 00:31:00.399
the first witness, there were four CFOs after I left all

00:31:00.419 --> 00:31:04.710
promoted up in the company, and they wanted me to be the

00:31:04.730 --> 00:31:07.819
first because they thought I was very professor-like, I

00:31:07.839 --> 00:31:11.839
guess it was the beard I don't know and that I could explain

00:31:11.859 --> 00:31:15.829
to the jury what street estimates were, what are earnings

00:31:15.849 --> 00:31:19.369
for share, what's the balance sheet, what's an income

00:31:19.389 --> 00:31:22.599
statement. And sure enough they put me on the stand and they

00:31:22.619 --> 00:31:28.309
had charts and it goes on almost all day and the jury is

00:31:28.329 --> 00:31:32.029
falling asleep. I am trying to make eye contact and they

00:31:32.049 --> 00:31:37.339
don't get it, they are bored, they don't understand it, they

00:31:37.359 --> 00:31:39.689
literally were falling asleep. I could have been teaching

00:31:39.709 --> 00:31:43.019
rocket science or brain surgery, they would pick up on it

00:31:43.039 --> 00:31:48.189
just as well what I was doing. Now this is big time

00:31:48.209 --> 00:31:52.079
courtroom drama. This was the first Sarbanes-Oxley case

00:31:52.099 --> 00:31:56.750
tried in the United States and it was a big deal. And you

00:31:56.769 --> 00:32:00.339
don't want to be involved in something like this if you don'

00:32:00.359 --> 00:32:03.919
t have to. Very first thing the lawyer gets in my face, he

00:32:03.939 --> 00:32:07.369
says Mr. Beam, you have had an extramarital affair, haven't

00:32:07.389 --> 00:32:12.849
you, and I said yes, because I had. The next day the

00:32:12.869 --> 00:32:16.970
Birmingham News, the newspaper said what a clever attorney

00:32:16.990 --> 00:32:22.589
he was to get me to admit to this. And the next day my wife

00:32:22.609 --> 00:32:25.359
wanted to come to the courtroom holding our son saying she

00:32:25.359 --> 00:32:29.539
still loved me and we have been married 40 years now and I

00:32:29.559 --> 00:32:33.139
have since discovered that he was on his fourth wife and the

00:32:33.159 --> 00:32:39.919
fourth wife is very much a trophy

00:32:39.939 --> 00:32:42.769
[inaudible]. He then gets in my face, he says Mr. Beam when

00:32:42.769 --> 00:32:45.519
you went to New York City and presented to investors, you

00:32:45.519 --> 00:32:48.349
lied, didn't you, when you presented the numbers to the

00:32:48.369 --> 00:32:52.919
board of directors you lied, Mr. Beam you lie so much, you

00:32:52.939 --> 00:32:57.210
don't know when you are telling the truth, you are a cheat

00:32:57.230 --> 00:33:01.269
and a liar. The jurors' eyes were this big, they were awake,

00:33:01.289 --> 00:33:04.369
they were having fun, this was entertainment to have this

00:33:04.389 --> 00:33:09.279
lawyer scream at me and call me a liar. The trial went on

00:33:09.299 --> 00:33:15.839
for six months, the jury deliberated for about 6 weeks, not

00:33:15.859 --> 00:33:20.659
guilty all charges. The legal community throughout the

00:33:20.679 --> 00:33:24.179
United States could not believe it. Enron, WorldCom, Tyco,

00:33:24.199 --> 00:33:28.240
Martha Stewart, they all went to prison but not Richard

00:33:28.259 --> 00:33:32.349
Scrushy. Within a couple of weeks he flew to Houston Texas

00:33:32.369 --> 00:33:36.649
to coach Ken Lay on how to conduct his trial at Enron, it

00:33:36.669 --> 00:33:41.929
was unbelievable. They asked the jury, some of the ones that

00:33:41.949 --> 00:33:46.029
were willing to talk, they asked them how did you find Mr.

00:33:46.049 --> 00:33:51.279
Scrushy not guilty when 17 people, 5 CFOs all said he was at

00:33:51.299 --> 00:33:55.109
the center of the fraud? And some of them said well it

00:33:55.129 --> 00:33:59.269
seemed like those CFOs were liars and Mr. Scrushy seemed

00:33:59.289 --> 00:34:05.129
like a nice Christian man. I cried like a baby when the

00:34:05.149 --> 00:34:07.970
verdict went out, I just could not believe that he had

00:34:07.990 --> 00:34:12.769
pulled it off. Three months later I was sent to a federal

00:34:12.789 --> 00:34:19.179
person and I got only three months and I don't understand

00:34:19.199 --> 00:34:21.440
why I got such a light sentence but I

00:34:21.440 --> 00:34:26.150
[inaudible] and I did not volunteer to do more time. I don't

00:34:26.170 --> 00:34:29.920
know if any of you all have been to prison but if you got to

00:34:29.920 --> 00:34:32.920
go to prison, you want to go to a federal minimum security

00:34:32.920 --> 00:34:36.860
prison. It's not that bad. My wife drove me there, I

00:34:36.880 --> 00:34:40.969
reported, it was on an air force base and you are not locked

00:34:40.989 --> 00:34:44.239
up per se and they actually tell you if you want to leave

00:34:44.239 --> 00:34:47.920
you can but they say when the marshals, US marshals catch

00:34:47.940 --> 00:34:51.139
you, you are going to big boy prison, and I didn't want to

00:34:51.139 --> 00:34:55.819
go to big boy prison so. Interesting place, prisons like

00:34:55.839 --> 00:34:58.639
this were initially set up I think for white collar

00:34:58.659 --> 00:35:03.179
criminals. And there were a lot of lawyers and accountants.

00:35:03.199 --> 00:35:07.079
There was, two beds down from me where we slept there was a

00:35:07.099 --> 00:35:11.549
thoracic surgeon that had cheated the Medicare problem. The

00:35:11.569 --> 00:35:15.539
most interesting guy I met while I was there was the father

00:35:15.559 --> 00:35:19.319
of the young man that just married Chelsea Clinton. He was a

00:35:19.339 --> 00:35:23.389
politician and he was in for seven years for I think

00:35:23.409 --> 00:35:26.909
embezzling the campaign funds or something, a nice guy, very

00:35:26.929 --> 00:35:31.029
interesting. About half of the prison thought was drug

00:35:31.049 --> 00:35:34.880
dealers. If you are not a non violent drug dealer and you

00:35:34.900 --> 00:35:38.069
get less than 10 years you can go to this kind of person.

00:35:38.069 --> 00:35:43.559
So, it was kind of a 50-50 white collar and drug dealers. I

00:35:43.579 --> 00:35:47.759
was there during football season and I am a big LSU fan and

00:35:47.779 --> 00:35:54.340
did I tell you, I think we were going to pay a ball game.

00:35:54.360 --> 00:35:57.860
One night LSU was playing and it was on TV and there is a

00:35:57.860 --> 00:36:00.610
television set in the lobby where you are housed and up

00:36:00.610 --> 00:36:03.360
until 10 o'clock at night you can watch television. So I

00:36:03.360 --> 00:36:06.519
went in to watch the game and there were a bunch of drug

00:36:06.519 --> 00:36:10.269
dealers and there they were watching the Miami Hurricane.

00:36:10.289 --> 00:36:13.329
And they called me pops, they said no pops, you are not

00:36:13.349 --> 00:36:16.029
watching your tigers, we are going to watch our

00:36:16.049 --> 00:36:18.690
[inaudible]. And I was depressed. I went around every

00:36:18.710 --> 00:36:22.460
barrack trying to find a TV which you are not supposed to do

00:36:22.480 --> 00:36:25.009
to watch the game and all the drug dealers had

00:36:25.029 --> 00:36:29.449
[inaudible]. The season ends, LSU gets an invitation to go

00:36:29.469 --> 00:36:33.759
to the Peach Bowl in Atlanta, Georgia, to play the Miami

00:36:33.779 --> 00:36:37.909
Hurricanes. So I said oh boy, I m going to get to see this

00:36:37.929 --> 00:36:41.340
game. The night before the game three of these guys came to

00:36:41.360 --> 00:36:44.579
me and they are biggest football players because they are in

00:36:44.599 --> 00:36:47.599
and out of prison and they lift weights and they are huge,

00:36:47.599 --> 00:36:51.980
and they ask me for my shoelaces, and I said why. They said

00:36:52.000 --> 00:36:55.360
so you won't hang yourself when Miami kicks LSU's ass

00:36:55.380 --> 00:37:00.179
tomorrow. So the game begins. It's me and 30 drug dealers

00:37:00.199 --> 00:37:06.099
from Miami and LSU wins 40 to 3 and it was absolutely the

00:37:06.119 --> 00:37:12.889
most fun you can possibly can while you are in prison. The

00:37:12.909 --> 00:37:16.989
worst thing about it there was nobody there with violence in

00:37:17.009 --> 00:37:19.759
their background. So the first night I took a shower I

00:37:19.779 --> 00:37:23.119
looked over my shoulder and nothing happened. But the worst

00:37:23.139 --> 00:37:27.029
part was the food. I happened to read the book Fast Food

00:37:27.049 --> 00:37:30.299
Nation while I was in there and it talked about how the very

00:37:30.319 --> 00:37:35.469
worst grade of all food goes to prisons. And after being

00:37:35.489 --> 00:37:38.880
there about a week I got the right to call my wife and I

00:37:38.880 --> 00:37:42.980
called her and I told her, I was telling her how bad the

00:37:43.000 --> 00:37:53.489
food was and she said Aaron, you are in prison. So I didn't

00:37:53.509 --> 00:37:56.509
complain about the food anymore. I got out in February and I

00:37:56.509 --> 00:38:00.739
am now a felon. Do you know what the job market is like for

00:38:00.759 --> 00:38:05.449
felons? It's not good. You can imagine in today's job market

00:38:05.469 --> 00:38:09.299
if there is a good job opening there's got to be 30, 40, 50

00:38:09.319 --> 00:38:13.819
people that qualify for applying. What chance does a felon

00:38:13.839 --> 00:38:20.759
have? So I needed a job and my fines and my legal bills were

00:38:20.779 --> 00:38:24.110
well in excess of a million dollars and I had to auction off

00:38:24.130 --> 00:38:28.799
my property and I needed a job. My wife had gone back to

00:38:28.819 --> 00:38:33.789
work. I had always liked gardening and working outdoors. And

00:38:33.809 --> 00:38:36.659
I went back to school and I got a two year certificate in

00:38:36.659 --> 00:38:40.880
turf management and for the past two years I have been

00:38:40.900 --> 00:38:46.029
making a living mowing lawns. It's not that bad except the

00:38:46.049 --> 00:38:51.960
heat in South Alabama during summer is just terrible. But

00:38:51.980 --> 00:38:58.949
when I was at HealthSouth and my salary was $500,000, I

00:38:58.969 --> 00:39:01.150
really didn't feel like I was earning that money, there were

00:39:01.150 --> 00:39:05.059
50,000 employees out there making that money for me. But

00:39:05.079 --> 00:39:08.699
today when I mow lawns and I cut somebody's grass and I get

00:39:08.719 --> 00:39:13.159
maybe $40 or $50 in South Alabama heat, I feel like I have

00:39:13.179 --> 00:39:18.059
really earned that money. I do sleep better now than I ever

00:39:18.079 --> 00:39:23.059
did when I was at HealthSouth. My wife is so smart she said

00:39:23.079 --> 00:39:25.829
Aaron, why don't you call the dean of the business school,

00:39:25.829 --> 00:39:29.329
over the years I have given them a lot of money, and see if

00:39:29.329 --> 00:39:32.079
you could talk to the MBA students about what has happened

00:39:32.079 --> 00:39:37.289
to you. And I do like talking so I called the dean and the

00:39:37.309 --> 00:39:41.039
dean said yeah, I think that would be a good idea. So, I

00:39:41.059 --> 00:39:45.079
went to talk to the MBA students, they had a special session

00:39:45.099 --> 00:39:49.159
for me and it went very well. I really felt like I had done

00:39:49.179 --> 00:39:54.199
something right. But I didn't think about doing speaking as

00:39:54.219 --> 00:40:00.089
a living, doing it for a living. Enron, WorldCom, Tyco,

00:40:00.109 --> 00:40:04.509
HealthSouth it all faded into the woodwork and they weren't

00:40:04.529 --> 00:40:08.839
front page news anymore. But something happened, you all

00:40:08.859 --> 00:40:12.750
might have heard about the Subprime Debacle. We learned that

00:40:12.769 --> 00:40:18.079
millions of loans were made to people, mortgage companies

00:40:18.099 --> 00:40:22.750
coached people how to lie on their loan applications to be

00:40:22.769 --> 00:40:26.400
able to buy a home they really couldn't afford. Wall Street

00:40:26.420 --> 00:40:30.150
took those loans, packaged to them, got them rated very

00:40:30.170 --> 00:40:32.349
highly, standard

00:40:32.369 --> 00:40:34.779
[inaudible] and they told the investing public these were

00:40:34.799 --> 00:40:38.380
good securities. On the other side of the house their hedge

00:40:38.400 --> 00:40:42.009
funds were short in these securities. The real estate bubble

00:40:42.029 --> 00:40:46.900
burst and all of a sudden, I mean just almost overnight, you

00:40:46.920 --> 00:40:50.449
have lived it, the largest insurance companies, the largest

00:40:50.469 --> 00:40:54.309
banks, the largest investment banking firms are all broke

00:40:54.329 --> 00:40:58.579
and they are asking for bailout and we are in the great

00:40:58.599 --> 00:41:06.110
recession. Why? At the heart of it is greed and unethical

00:41:06.130 --> 00:41:10.369
business behavior. HealthSouth was bad but this was massive

00:41:10.389 --> 00:41:15.429
so massive that it put the whole world into a recession, the

00:41:15.449 --> 00:41:21.339
great recession. When Webster first published its

00:41:21.359 --> 00:41:25.460
dictionary, he defined success as being generous,

00:41:25.480 --> 00:41:30.299
prosperous, healthy and kind. Today, that definition is the

00:41:30.319 --> 00:41:36.110
attainment of wealth, fame and rank. Having a bigger house

00:41:36.130 --> 00:41:39.389
and more cars than your neighbor and you are up to here in

00:41:39.409 --> 00:41:44.239
debt is not being successful particularly if you accumulate

00:41:44.259 --> 00:41:50.360
that wealth by doing unethical business things. So I have

00:41:50.380 --> 00:41:54.719
been speaking to colleges and companies and associations,

00:41:54.739 --> 00:42:00.059
fraud associations and accounting groups about ethics. And

00:42:00.079 --> 00:42:03.589
you might say and I am sure you have said this, you can't

00:42:03.609 --> 00:42:07.750
teach ethic, you are either ethical or you are not. I don't

00:42:07.769 --> 00:42:10.319
believe it's just black or white, I believe it's like the

00:42:10.319 --> 00:42:14.799
pilot that landed that plane on the Hudson River. He studied

00:42:14.819 --> 00:42:19.889
every crash, every water landing and he knew in those

00:42:19.909 --> 00:42:24.170
critical moments exactly what to do and how to bring that

00:42:24.190 --> 00:42:27.339
plane in and save those people's lives. I think today if we

00:42:27.339 --> 00:42:31.960
teach ethics at high schools, colleges, on the job, you will

00:42:31.980 --> 00:42:35.210
be better prepared to deal with the kind of things I had to

00:42:35.210 --> 00:42:40.139
deal with and trust me, you will when you get out into the

00:42:40.159 --> 00:42:45.469
business world, you will face things like I faced maybe not

00:42:45.489 --> 00:42:50.619
as big but you will be asked to keep sales open a little

00:42:50.639 --> 00:42:52.159
longer

00:42:52.179 --> 00:42:55.589
[inaudible] maybe cut payables off a little early to help

00:42:55.609 --> 00:42:58.960
the bottom line. I think the image of the accountant with a

00:42:58.980 --> 00:43:05.009
green visor in the backroom is for a large part true. The

00:43:05.029 --> 00:43:07.529
card charging people are in sales, they are in marketing,

00:43:07.529 --> 00:43:10.880
they are out front they like confrontation. Accountants like

00:43:10.900 --> 00:43:14.599
to kind of be in the background and please people by keeping

00:43:14.619 --> 00:43:18.969
a nice set of books, and that hurts you. The largest

00:43:18.989 --> 00:43:23.480
turnover of executives in the United States is CFOs. CFO of

00:43:23.500 --> 00:43:28.900
public companies only last 31/2 years and part of it is

00:43:28.920 --> 00:43:35.019
because they are constantly asked to make the numbers what

00:43:35.039 --> 00:43:39.250
they need to be and it's really, really a big problem and

00:43:39.269 --> 00:43:42.949
you need to be aware of that. When you go to work for a

00:43:42.969 --> 00:43:46.509
company and you figure out you have got a sociopath at the

00:43:46.529 --> 00:43:47.900
top

00:43:47.920 --> 00:43:51.199
[inaudible] and the tone of the top is all wrong, you need

00:43:51.219 --> 00:43:58.359
to leave that company and go to another company. There is a

00:43:58.379 --> 00:44:02.519
professor at Arizona State University Dr. Marianne Jennings

00:44:02.539 --> 00:44:06.429
and she is probably one of the outstanding ethics professors

00:44:06.449 --> 00:44:09.679
in United States and she has written a book called the Seven

00:44:09.699 --> 00:44:13.920
Signs of Ethical Collapse and the book is about Enron,

00:44:13.940 --> 00:44:18.199
WorldCom, Tyco and HealthSouth. And she talks about the

00:44:18.219 --> 00:44:22.579
seven things to look for in a company before it has a

00:44:22.599 --> 00:44:27.079
complete ethical meltdown. The first sign is the pressure to

00:44:27.099 --> 00:44:30.119
maintain the numbers, make the numbers, and at HealthSouth

00:44:30.139 --> 00:44:34.559
that was our whole focus make the numbers no matter what,

00:44:34.579 --> 00:44:38.779
make the numbers. But if the culture is such that it's make

00:44:38.799 --> 00:44:43.250
the numbers with no regard for doing it ethically, doing it

00:44:43.269 --> 00:44:48.219
correctly, that's the first sign. The second sign is fear

00:44:48.239 --> 00:44:54.089
and silence. Richard managed by intimidation and most people

00:44:54.109 --> 00:44:57.989
don't want to tell their boss, they are afraid they will

00:44:58.009 --> 00:45:03.289
lose their job so there is a culture of fear and silence in

00:45:03.309 --> 00:45:07.679
these companies. You might think my whole speech today is

00:45:07.699 --> 00:45:10.549
bashing of Richard Scrushy which you have got to realize

00:45:10.569 --> 00:45:14.130
that when you have an Enron or WorldCom or HealthSouth, the

00:45:14.150 --> 00:45:17.949
guy at the top, the CEO is probably a bigger than life

00:45:17.969 --> 00:45:22.779
personality who hires young people or people that he can

00:45:22.799 --> 00:45:28.319
control and manage and intimidate and get them to do what he

00:45:28.339 --> 00:45:33.349
wants. The fourth sign is weak board of directors and this

00:45:33.369 --> 00:45:37.230
is probably the most important, certainly back in the

00:45:37.250 --> 00:45:41.159
HealthSouth era. Dr. Jennings said that HealthSouth had the

00:45:41.179 --> 00:45:45.059
weakest board of directors she had ever seen. Just

00:45:45.079 --> 00:45:49.599
absolutely any time Richard put somebody on the board and

00:45:49.619 --> 00:45:52.869
they gave him any trouble he just got rid of them. So over

00:45:52.869 --> 00:45:57.400
the years he just developed a board of yes men. The board at

00:45:57.420 --> 00:46:01.460
WorldCom was referred to as Bernie's Boys, Bernie Ebbers was

00:46:01.480 --> 00:46:06.769
the chairman of the board and these were the boys. Conflict,

00:46:06.789 --> 00:46:10.219
Richard made sure we all had stock options, we were given

00:46:10.239 --> 00:46:14.349
loans to exercise our stock options, we did business with

00:46:14.369 --> 00:46:18.009
our board members. We bought $8 million worth of glass from

00:46:18.029 --> 00:46:22.549
a guy to go into our hospitals who was on the board. So

00:46:22.569 --> 00:46:27.880
Richard set us up to be conflicted so we would stay true to

00:46:27.900 --> 00:46:34.849
the calls. Innovation like no other; I would challenge

00:46:34.869 --> 00:46:37.949
anybody in this room to explain to us what Enron really did,

00:46:37.969 --> 00:46:41.529
nobody understood Enron's business, they had this unique

00:46:41.549 --> 00:46:45.210
business. The unique thing like no other thing at

00:46:45.230 --> 00:46:48.329
HealthSouth was our earnings. For 10 years, we were

00:46:48.349 --> 00:46:54.659
perfectly straight no bumps at all. People sometime look

00:46:54.679 --> 00:46:58.519
hard at companies when they are doing badly but all of these

00:46:58.539 --> 00:47:02.109
companies Enron, HealthSouth, they were high flying

00:47:02.129 --> 00:47:09.699
companies apparently doing very well, too well. The last

00:47:09.719 --> 00:47:15.489
sign is goodness in some areas atones for evil in other

00:47:15.509 --> 00:47:21.019
areas. It's kind of an interesting sign but it's there in

00:47:21.039 --> 00:47:24.989
many cases. HealthSouth gave lots of money to churches and

00:47:25.009 --> 00:47:28.409
schools and everything. There were many, many things

00:47:28.429 --> 00:47:30.839
throughout the State of Alabama with Richard Scrushy's name

00:47:30.839 --> 00:47:35.559
on it, it was HealthSouth money but he made sure he gave

00:47:35.579 --> 00:47:41.190
money to these causes and he was recognized as this very

00:47:41.210 --> 00:47:46.440
generous person. One last Richard Scrushy story because it's

00:47:46.460 --> 00:47:50.710
the best one, Richard has had three wives and nine children

00:47:50.730 --> 00:47:56.130
and his third wife was a graduate of Troy University in

00:47:56.150 --> 00:48:00.619
South Alabama. And they wanted to get into division one

00:48:00.639 --> 00:48:05.309
football and they needed to sell x number of season tickets

00:48:05.329 --> 00:48:08.369
and they needed to enlarge their stadium. So HealthSouth

00:48:08.389 --> 00:48:11.049
bought the tickets and gave them the money to enlarge the

00:48:11.049 --> 00:48:13.980
stadium and sure enough it became the Richard M. Scrushy

00:48:14.000 --> 00:48:21.699
stadium. But he also required that the band play his song,

00:48:21.719 --> 00:48:32.319
Honk if you Like the Honky Tonk, unbelievable. I have

00:48:32.339 --> 00:48:35.420
written a book called HealthSouth: The Wagon to Disaster and

00:48:35.440 --> 00:48:38.989
you might say oh look at Aaron Beam, he is up here preaching

00:48:39.009 --> 00:48:42.509
to us about ethics and he is trying to get rich off of a

00:48:42.509 --> 00:48:45.609
book he wrote. I don't know how many of you have written a

00:48:45.609 --> 00:48:49.980
book. You might have doctor but the chances of you writing a

00:48:50.000 --> 00:48:54.549
book and you becoming wealthy from it is about as likely as

00:48:54.569 --> 00:48:57.069
you being struck by lightening while you are on your

00:48:57.069 --> 00:49:02.150
honeymoon with Cindy Crawford, this is not going to happen.

00:49:02.170 --> 00:49:05.650
My speech is a condensed version of the book and that's the

00:49:05.670 --> 00:49:09.139
reason I wrote the book. I hope universities will have

00:49:09.159 --> 00:49:12.719
students read it so that they can understand how this all

00:49:12.739 --> 00:49:17.469
evolved, how I got on the slippery slope and how I made the

00:49:17.489 --> 00:49:22.119
terrible decisions that I did. Today, I am a felon, that's

00:49:22.139 --> 00:49:25.819
all I am ever going to be remembered as. I am not going to

00:49:25.839 --> 00:49:28.859
be remembered as a guy that started one of the largest

00:49:28.879 --> 00:49:30.980
health care companies, involved in the largest companies in

00:49:30.980 --> 00:49:34.859
Alabama, I am going to be known as a guy that cooked the

00:49:34.879 --> 00:49:39.980
books of HealthSouth. You do not want to go there. All the

00:49:40.000 --> 00:49:44.199
unethical behavior is very corrosive, it's like metal

00:49:44.219 --> 00:49:47.940
rusting. One day it rusts more and more and more and the

00:49:47.960 --> 00:49:51.289
bridge falls down and people die. If you let yourself

00:49:51.309 --> 00:49:55.179
compromise your ethnical principles, it will lead to a very

00:49:55.199 --> 00:49:59.460
bad ending and that's the message I want to bring today. At

00:49:59.480 --> 00:50:02.369
this point I would like to throw it open to questions. Does

00:50:02.369 --> 00:50:06.429
anybody have any question?

00:50:06.449 --> 00:50:07.449
[Audience]

00:50:07.449 --> 00:50:13.250
He is in prison, believe it or not. This was his mug shot

00:50:13.269 --> 00:50:20.279
when he went to prison. After the government lost the case

00:50:20.299 --> 00:50:23.639
of HealthSouth he was indicted for bribing the Governor of

00:50:23.659 --> 00:50:27.849
Alabama. The governor was running for reelection while he

00:50:27.869 --> 00:50:30.960
was at HealthSouth and he gave the governor or HealthSouth

00:50:30.980 --> 00:50:38.690
gave the governor $500,000 for his campaign funds. Richard

00:50:38.710 --> 00:50:42.529
got appointed to a board, a CON Certificate of Need board, a

00:50:42.549 --> 00:50:46.989
public state position and the federal government said this

00:50:47.009 --> 00:50:51.829
was a bribe. The legal community said this is silly, people

00:50:51.849 --> 00:50:55.859
give politicians money all the time and it's seldom ever

00:50:55.879 --> 00:51:00.329
considered a bribe, it is just not. Well Richard fired up

00:51:00.349 --> 00:51:03.449
his TV show, he got the bibles back out, got the ministers

00:51:03.469 --> 00:51:08.859
in the courtroom and he was found guilty. He is in a prison

00:51:08.879 --> 00:51:12.989
is Beaumont Texas now and he has been there for 3 years and

00:51:13.009 --> 00:51:17.369
he is doing seven years. I think he got one year knocked

00:51:17.389 --> 00:51:21.809
off. They have asked him what is he going to do when he gets

00:51:21.829 --> 00:51:25.079
out and he said he is going to continue preaching and he is

00:51:25.079 --> 00:51:31.629
going to write and sing Christian music so you all get in

00:51:31.649 --> 00:51:34.899
line for that album when it comes out. I have had no contact

00:51:34.899 --> 00:51:39.420
with him, don't want to. I hope he lives by the rules you

00:51:39.440 --> 00:51:41.539
can't own a gun when you are a felon.

00:51:41.539 --> 00:51:42.539
[Audience]

00:51:42.539 --> 00:51:59.029
Well we were in a bubble, a stock market bubble in

00:51:59.049 --> 00:52:04.829
particularly health care stocks and part of it was just like

00:52:04.849 --> 00:52:09.879
the mortgage thing, you rationalize that it's okay.

00:52:09.899 --> 00:52:10.899
[Audience]

00:52:10.899 --> 00:52:16.710
No, and it really didn't, maybe toward the end it got down

00:52:16.730 --> 00:52:19.480
to patient care level but it was pretty much in the

00:52:19.480 --> 00:52:24.460
boardroom. And the day we did it, we cooked the books, you

00:52:24.480 --> 00:52:27.879
don't think about the consequences, you don't look ahead and

00:52:27.899 --> 00:52:31.829
think oh my God this is going to turn out very terrible. You

00:52:31.849 --> 00:52:36.039
rationalize it, you believe you are doing the best choice,

00:52:36.059 --> 00:52:40.690
you are picking the best option about doing what you do.

00:52:40.710 --> 00:52:44.719
White collar criminals are like that. I met an FBI agent at

00:52:44.739 --> 00:52:47.859
a conference the other day and he was very clear, the

00:52:47.879 --> 00:52:52.779
typical criminal that holds up a bank with a gun, the money

00:52:52.799 --> 00:52:56.129
is in the bank and he wants it, okay; the white collar

00:52:56.149 --> 00:53:01.349
criminal though many times rationalizes and convinces

00:53:01.369 --> 00:53:05.900
himself that what you are doing is not really bad and if you

00:53:05.920 --> 00:53:16.759
can pull if off, what the heck.

00:53:16.779 --> 00:53:17.779
[Audience]

00:53:17.779 --> 00:53:21.730
All of our accounting was centralized so people sent all

00:53:21.750 --> 00:53:24.289
their financial information, payrolls and everything was

00:53:24.309 --> 00:53:28.549
done centrally. So Bill Owens was a very smart guy and he

00:53:28.569 --> 00:53:32.750
was able to do it. How he pulled it off for all those years

00:53:32.769 --> 00:53:36.569
I don't know. The forensic accounting bill to clean up the

00:53:36.589 --> 00:53:41.099
books was $643 million dollars so there were literally

00:53:41.119 --> 00:53:45.339
hundreds of thousands of bogus entries made. How the

00:53:45.359 --> 00:53:50.409
auditors did not catch it, I don't know. I wasn't surprised

00:53:50.429 --> 00:53:52.909
that they didn't catch it the first year I was there but how

00:53:52.909 --> 00:53:58.699
it went on, the last audited balance sheet had 300 million

00:53:58.719 --> 00:54:02.920
in cash that didn't exist. So they weren't real good

00:54:02.940 --> 00:54:19.569
auditors.

00:54:19.589 --> 00:54:20.589
[Audience]

00:54:20.589 --> 00:54:25.079
You really should. Certainly if you know it's fraudulent,

00:54:25.099 --> 00:54:28.769
you know it's fraud, you should report it and you could be

00:54:28.789 --> 00:54:31.579
rewarded handsomely. The new

00:54:31.599 --> 00:54:35.239
[inaudible] bill, I think you get 30% of the fine that

00:54:35.259 --> 00:54:41.219
ultimately goes down so. There is a lot of controversy

00:54:41.239 --> 00:54:43.710
around that whether or not that's a good piece of

00:54:43.710 --> 00:54:46.500
legislation or not but you certainly should, you should

00:54:46.519 --> 00:54:52.079
tell. But it takes a lot of courage. The easy thing to do is

00:54:52.099 --> 00:54:57.929
just walk away but if you know for sure that there is out

00:54:57.949 --> 00:55:07.900
and there is hotlines and ways you can do it and not be

00:55:07.920 --> 00:55:08.920
known.

00:55:08.920 --> 00:55:09.920
[Audience]

00:55:09.920 --> 00:55:17.819
The 2010 Aaron Beam is a lot smarter than he was back then.

00:55:17.839 --> 00:55:18.839
[Audience]

00:55:18.839 --> 00:55:21.779
I would have said no Richard we can't do this, this is

00:55:21.779 --> 00:55:24.940
pushing the envelope too far, this is fraud, this is wrong.

00:55:24.960 --> 00:55:29.029
And he would have fired me, I would have lost my job but I

00:55:29.049 --> 00:55:35.039
should have done that and I would do it today. You get

00:55:35.059 --> 00:55:43.429
religion when you go to prison.

00:55:43.449 --> 00:55:44.449
[Audience]

00:55:44.449 --> 00:55:50.529
The trick with a person like Richard is that the personality

00:55:50.549 --> 00:55:55.519
type of a CEO, a real hard charging CEO has a lot of the

00:55:55.539 --> 00:55:58.879
traits that Richard has. You just need to size them up and

00:55:58.899 --> 00:56:02.150
make sure that they are not a sociopath because clearly if

00:56:02.170 --> 00:56:05.129
Richard can sue me for anything is probably me calling him

00:56:05.149 --> 00:56:09.569
sociopath but he is, he has no conscience, and you have to

00:56:09.589 --> 00:56:14.789
figure that out. But you look at the people, look at Donald

00:56:14.809 --> 00:56:18.059
Trump, I mean he loves himself, he loves being on reality

00:56:18.079 --> 00:56:22.449
TV, everybody wants to go to work for Donald. I am not

00:56:22.469 --> 00:56:27.289
saying Donald Trump is Richard Scrushy but Richard had the

00:56:27.309 --> 00:56:39.900
Donald Trump type personality of loving himself.

00:56:39.920 --> 00:56:40.920
[Audience]

00:56:40.920 --> 00:56:45.139
Bill Owens got 5 years, Weston Smith who actually wrote

00:56:45.159 --> 00:56:50.909
chapter 10 in my book he got two years, Mike Martin the CFO

00:56:50.929 --> 00:56:54.789
that replaced me got 3 years but really kind of life

00:56:54.809 --> 00:57:00.730
sentences. If you plead, if you come forward and plead then

00:57:00.750 --> 00:57:03.710
the government does have to take you to court ,you do get a

00:57:03.710 --> 00:57:08.859
lot better deal. All of those guys, myself, if I had tried

00:57:08.879 --> 00:57:11.779
to lie through it and they had found me guilty, I probably

00:57:11.779 --> 00:57:14.279
would have gone to prison for many, many, many years.

00:57:14.279 --> 00:57:15.279
[Audience]

00:57:14.279 --> 00:57:15.279
Okay thank you.

